window.docContents[192].resolve({"/#Jakarta-Persistence--3___-Entity-Operations--3___6___-Entity-Instance-Lifecycle--3___6___7___-Refreshing-an-Entity-Instance":{"contents":"The state of a managed entity instance is refreshed from the database when it is passed as an argument to the refresh() method of EntityManager or when the refresh operation cascades from another instance to the managed instance.\n\nLet e be an instance of an entity class. If e is passed as the first argument to the refresh() method of EntityManager, or if the refresh operation is applied to e via cascade:\n\n* if e is a managed entity, the state of e is refreshed from the database by reading the record represented by e and modifying the state of e to match, overwriting any pending modifications made to the entity; or* if e is a new, detached, or removed entity, the IllegalArgumentException is thrown.* If e is managed, the refresh operation is also applied to every entity t referenced by a relationship owned by e and annotated with the cascade element value cascade=REFRESH or cascade=ALL or mapped as such by the equivalent XML descriptor element.\n\n\n\nFormalized SpecificationOnly a managed entity may successfully refresh.\nRefresh reloads fields from the database, but neither fields nor database\ncontents are part of our state. The action therefore leaves the modeled state\nunchanged.Its only requirement is that the instance exists and is managed. With no\nassignments in the body, this is an allowed step that preserves every value.action Refresh (e : entity) {\n  require created e ∧ e_phase e = managed\n}\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Jakarta Persistence\u00093. Entity Operations\u00093.6. Entity Instance Lifecycle","header":"3.6.7. Refreshing an Entity Instance","id":"/#Jakarta-Persistence--3___-Entity-Operations--3___6___-Entity-Instance-Lifecycle--3___6___7___-Refreshing-an-Entity-Instance"}});